A||N||22||Seanh||Is this bad/hard on the engine?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||05-22-2001||07:14 PM||||I was just doing some racing last night with the stereo system off, so I couldn't help but listening to the engine. It honestly sounds really good when I wrap it hard like that. I am good at mechanics and all that stuff, but not really as inteligent about the background of it I guess. When I race, and stomp on the gas right off the line, WOT all the way to the end, is that really hard on my engine? Or tanny? My 3.4 seems to really enjoy it, but I'm just wonderin' how much damage I'm doing to it. I run at a steady 170 deg. when I'm racing BTW. I run full synthetic Mobile One 5w30 usally, but I'm going to change to 10w30 because I'v notice low oil pressure/warning light when sitting at a light that I don't notice when the temp outside is colder. Any other reccomendations for brands/products to protect the engine under hard driving conditions? Thanks<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 05-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||05-22-2001||07:33 PM||||Bob Steiger recommended Mobil 1 10-30 oil in my new 3.4L engine.  I haven't pushed my new engine too much, but that type of driving is hard on the engine and the transmission.  How hard depends.  Race cars driving at red line, last for 500-600 miles, then the mechanics tear them down for rebuild.  If you are doing it all the time, expect a shortend engine and transmission life.  Dragster cars last for even much shorter time.<P>If you enjoy driving like that and have the skills and/or money for rebuilds, by all means, go for it.  Auto racing is an expensive hobby.<P>Advice:  Don't drive your transportation car to the race track.  If something blows, you have no way home.||||reg||3
Z||000002||rogergarrison||05-22-2001||07:54 PM||||small cube engines love revs (Porsche,Ferrari) bigger put out more torque at much lower rpm. My limiter is set for 7300 and I hit it sometimes. Still going strong after 80K. No oil use, no noises. Lots better than lugging it around in too high a gear.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||05-22-2001||10:41 PM||||You didn't put a 3.4 in it to last 250,000 miles, did you? I'm betting you put a 3.4 in it to have fun with your right foot. Have fun! Just don't over-rev it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Taijiguy||05-22-2001||10:46 PM||||Driving it like that is OK, but motors hate neon &lt;snicker&gt;  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000005||Steve Normington||05-23-2001||12:22 AM||||As long as you aren't dumping the clutch on takeoff and you don't lug the engine, this kind of driving shouldn't damage anything. Cars are a lot tougher that people think. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||05-23-2001||12:27 AM||||My 3.4L Fiero has an 4 speed Auto in her. The new 87GT has a 3 speed auto. I want a manual so bad its starting to piss me off. I just can't find any in good condition for a good price. I was going to dump the 4spd auto when I did my 3.4, but I decided to stick with the auto in the end. Kinda wish I would have switched it though.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||05-23-2001||12:37 AM||||Funny you should bring up "not driving your transportation car to the track". A couple weeks back I drove my 83 Camaro Z28 up to a track about 30 miles away. I was timing QM runs in it, and lost a rocker arm. It in turn (running at like 6k RPM) tore the valve cover right off and threw the pushrod out. I started driving it home right then and made it about half way back when the oil pressure just dropped out on me so I pulled over. Dead Cell phone, no more oil, V7 engine missing a valve cover, I was pretty screwed. Another guy comming home from the track stoped by on his way back about 2 hours later, and I hitched a ride home.<P>Needless to say, good advice.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||05-23-2001||08:52 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steve Normington:<BR><B>As long as you aren't dumping the clutch on takeoff and you don't lug the engine, this kind of driving shouldn't damage anything. Cars are a lot tougher that people think. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What's lugging the engine supposed to do?<BR>Low RPM, high load is the way to get good gas mileage. RPM is what wears engines out, not load.||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||05-23-2001||09:43 AM||||Not true. Lugging-wide open throttle in too high a gear, low rpm=detonation. Cars driven on hiway are in much better condition than ones driven around town, not to mention all the deposits that dont get burned off in town traffic. Try driving up a long hill in high gear and giving it more gas. Youll start bucking and making all kinds of funny noises. At the top guess wholl blow up first. Orief or someone can explain it better than me, so maybe they will jump in. Ill give up a few mpg to not have to buy an engine every 10k.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||05-23-2001||09:46 AM||||When you lug you engine you have the lowest oil pressure possible (cause the oil pump is turning slow) and the highest pressure and strain possible onthe bearings - also more likely to get preignition and pinging.<P>  Its ok to drive around at 1500 rpms to get good mileage, but when you want to speed up or take on a hill, downshift before you give it a lot of gas.||||reg||1
Z||000011||SteveJ||05-23-2001||10:31 AM||||Oh, lugging is bad, bad, bad. The older engines from 1900 to the 1960 could take some lugging without problems but all modern engines have problems with it. It has to do with the length of the stroke used. I don't know of any current engines that could be considered long stroke engines. Todays engines are short stroke engines that enjoy high rpm's. We are talking here about the ratio of the bore to the stroke. Engines that are "over square" ( bore is greater than stroke) enjoy high rpm's.<BR>My grandfather used to tell of slow races they used to run where the cars were put in high gear and then run to see who could go the slowest down Main street. You would be absolutly nuts to try that today, but with their old long stroke engines they got away with it then. And I think my terms are correct, They got away with it then, it was not a good idea even then. You must remember that those engines needed rebuilding at 80,000 miles due to ring and bearing problems. Likely they shortened the life of their engines but the engines wore out so fast anyway they didn't notice the additional damage.<P>Gotta love todays high rpm engines. They give faster response and can pull over a wider rpm range but shouldn't be abused by lugging or running over the red line.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fierowrecker||05-23-2001||10:21 PM||||I always drive the transportation car to the track, I TOW the race car!!!<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ken Wittlief||05-24-2001||09:52 AM||||I hate to admit this now, when I was 19 my 'employer' had a 1 ton dodge pickup for a few weeks while the dodge van was being repaired at the GSA motorpool.<P>  It had a 4 speed manual transmission. Just to see what would happen I started off in first gear and got going about 2mph, then shifted into 4th, let the clutch out, and floored it.<P>   That darned thing went PUTT PUTT PUTT PUTT PUTT PUTT PUTT at about 1 second per cylinder fire, and held a steady 3mph down the street. I couldnt belive that thing would run at about 30 rpm (V8) but it did.<P>   Now I cant believe I ever did anything so stupid to a brand new Mopar truck. It didnt seem to do it any harm, but even still :-O||||reg||1
Z||000014||Will||05-24-2001||08:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>When you lug you engine you have the lowest oil pressure possible (cause the oil pump is turning slow) and the highest pressure and strain possible onthe bearings - also more likely to get preignition and pinging.<P>  Its ok to drive around at 1500 rpms to get good mileage, but when you want to speed up or take on a hill, downshift before you give it a lot of gas.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>If the engine is tuned properly, it won't detonate under these conditions. Even a non-EFI engine.<P>Improperly tuned engines detonate in this mode of operation because cylinder pressure is highest at low RPM, high load.<P>Has anyone besides me actually sat down and figured out how much acceleration a piston undergoes?<P>The bearing load imposed by engine torque is minuscule compared to the bearing load imposed by reversing the direction of the piston.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||gixxer||05-24-2001||09:10 PM||||Bravo!<BR>Stick to your guns, Will...<BR>--A question posed to the Road and Track Technical Correspondence column, Aug. '82.<BR>Titled-"LUGGING AND REVVING" <BR>"...Could the economy technique of shifting early and using large throttle opening, so much these days, have this bad side effect?(worn main bearings)"<BR>The R&T answer is...<BR>"...The place to look for any possible effects of lugging would be the rod bearings, not the mains. But this has not been a problem with the vast majority of production cars since the days of Porches with roller bearings. It was these cars, in fact along with some of the woefully underdesigned British sports-car engines of the Fifties, where the fabled fear of lugging originated."<BR>"Your best rule of thumb is always to select the highest gear in which your engine will deliver the power you need while running smoothly...."<BR>Compared to plain bearings, roller and ball bearings have a very low load capacity.<BR>--And Seanh,---"I'v notice the the low oil pressure/warning light when sitting at a light..." It sounds to me Sean, that your engine may be a bit worn. It might be a good time to start babying it. I'd investigate and find out what the oil pressure really is.<BR>  <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Seanh||05-24-2001||09:17 PM||||I highly doubt its worn since its a fresh rebuilt engine. I am thinking its because I am running synthetic 5w30 because it ran with prefect oil pressure when I first start it up and during winter.||||reg||1
Z||000017||fiero56||05-24-2001||09:33 PM||||5W30 is what the engine is supposed to run on. If you're having low oil presssure problems with the recommended oil on a new engine, tolerances/clerances are not correct somewhere. Bearing are the biggest factor in oil pressue. On a rebuild of most GM 60* engines, it is adviseable to leave an extra .001" on crank and rod journals. A high pressure huigh volume oil pump is also generally recommended for the engines when rebuilt. I've been researching a $hit load about clearances for my 2.8 project.<P>10w30 is also the same weight when warm as 5w30, so you will see no improvement in hot oil pressure. You will have thicker oil at startup though...||||reg||1
Z||000018||Seanh||05-24-2001||09:41 PM||||Now I feel real stupid! I guess it really never hit me that it will be the same warm weight. What should the oil pressure be? Accoding to the Haynes it should be like 36-40 at 2k RPM, which mine runs every time I check it. However when the engine is warm and I stop at a light it will drop to right about the red area, and sometimes jump into the red, but it doesn't stay very consistant and never stays in the red more then a couple seconds.<P>So should I maybe try some 5w40 or 10w40?<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 05-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||gixxer||05-24-2001||09:53 PM||||Usually people put an extra .001" clearance in ("just to be safe") and run into low oil pressure problems. Then they have to run a high volume pump to get the pressure up, which consumes more HP.<BR>Amateur builders (me included) have been doing this and screwing it all up since Christ was a corporal.<BR>The solution is to run the clearances that Chevy recommends.<BR>Glad to hear your engine is new Seanh. If it was old, the low oil pressure you described would be indicative of a degenerative bearing condition  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <BR>  ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Seanh||05-24-2001||10:04 PM||||I built it (I hope) exatly to specs with no extra clearances, that I know of that is. Any other ideas on what could cause this oil pressure crap? Maybe simply a bad sending unit?||||reg||1
Z||000021||fiero56||05-24-2001||11:21 PM||||I'm running 10w40 in my Formula. But it has over 175K miles on the engine, and with 5w30 it sat near red at idle when hot, but never flickered the light, and had slight rod knock at low rpm with load. I started using the heavier oil more than 15K miles ago.<BR>My v-6 SE, occasionally flickers the light with 5w30, but the sender is bad. To be safe until I replace the sender, I am running 10w40 in it also. What sucks, is Molil doesn't make a synthetic 10w40. So I have been using the blends with nice results. <P>I would suggest getting a mechanical oil pressue gauge and see what an accurate reading is. Minimum acceptable oil pressure at idle is 15psi, and 30psi at 2K rpm. If the light is flickering and you don't hear valve-train clicking or other, it is most likely the sender. The other thing I have noticed, is the red area for oil light on my Formula is about half the size it is on my SE. I wonder if Pontiac changed low oil pressure warning light pressures somewhere in Fiero production.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Seanh||05-24-2001||11:26 PM||||I am wondering, in my 82 Camaro, I run like 50-60 at idle and 80 when driving. That was kinda why my Fiero had me concerned with the lower oil pressure, but the Camaro also has a 350 in it.||||reg||1
